message: If you use physverify in your work, please cite it as below.
title: "physverify: code verification for numerical simulation"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Giri
    given-names: Ram Chandra
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0008-7149-7938"
version: 0.10.0
date-released: 2026-11-27
license: MIT
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.23534512
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.23534512
    description: Concept DOI, all versions (Zenodo)
repository-code: https://github.com/rcgiri-physics/physverify
url: https://rcgiri-physics.github.io/physverify/
abstract: >-
  physverify is a tool for code verification of numerical simulation: it tests
  whether simulation code behaves as the mathematics predicts. It checks
  convergence order, agreement with exact or manufactured solutions,
  conservation laws and budgets, and time-integration coefficients
  (Runge-Kutta, IMEX and Rosenbrock order conditions, dense output and
  stability). It reports PASS, FAIL,
  EXACT or INCONCLUSIVE with the numbers, and runs from Python, the command
  line, pytest or GitHub Actions.
keywords:
  - verification
  - method of manufactured solutions
  - order of accuracy
  - numerical methods
  - physics
  - simulation
  - testing
